From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] ksmbd: sync fs/smb/server with 6.18.52 stable

Bring the in-kernel SMB server from 6.18.39 to the 6.18.52 stable state,
taken verbatim as the difference between the two kernel.org release
tarballs for fs/smb/server plus the get_rfc1002_len() move into
fs/smb/common/cifsglob.h that the server change depends on. The SMB
client is not touched.

Main reason: on 6.18.39 every SMB session that opens a file leaks its
TCP socket. The last reference to a ksmbd_conn can be dropped by
__free_opinfo() from an RCU callback, whose bare kfree() skips releasing
the transport (upstream b1f1e80620de "ksmbd: centralize ksmbd_conn final
release to plug transport leak"). Measured on a GL-BE9300: 200 SMB
sessions doing put/get/rm left 200 TCPv6 socket objects behind; a night
of client traffic grew TCPv6 slab to 373 MB and OOM-killed the box.
Also carries the stable fixes for IPC message bounds checks, connection
abort on failure and dead-peer detection.

Drop this patch when the target moves to a kernel >= 6.18.52.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
